# CollabHub Auth — manual API walkthrough
#
# For the VS Code REST Client extension (humao.rest-client). "Send Request" appears
# above each request; run them top to bottom, because most of them feed the next one
# by referencing an earlier response body — no copying tokens by hand.
#
# Assumes the stack is up:
#
#     docker compose up -d postgres redis-cache auth
#
# Auth is on 8001 (AUTH_PORT in .env). It needs Postgres and Redis Cache (R1) and
# nothing else — no Messaging, no Garage, no Elasticsearch.
#
# Two things worth knowing before you start:
#
# * Refresh tokens rotate. Each one works exactly once, so re-running a request out
#   of order will 401 — that is the design, not a broken file. Sign in again to get
#   a fresh pair.
# * Access tokens live 15 minutes and are signed with a key generated at startup
#   unless AUTH_SIGNING_KEY is set. Restart the container and every token before it
#   becomes invalid.

@host = http://localhost:8001
@json = application/json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Health
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Liveness — is the process up. Checks nothing.
GET {{host}}/health/live

### Readiness — checks Postgres and Redis Cache. 503 until both answer.
GET {{host}}/health/ready

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JWKS — how the rest of the platform trusts these tokens
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### The public keys. Every other service fetches this and verifies signatures
# locally, so no request on the platform is ever routed through Auth.
# `kid` is an RFC 7638 thumbprint, so it is the same in every replica.
# Cacheable on purpose — check the Cache-Control header on the response.
GET {{host}}/.well-known/jwks.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing in
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Local-only sign-in: any email, no password, no IdP.
# First use creates the account, a workspace it owns, and membership of the
# shared "CollabHub Demo" workspace. This route does not exist unless
# APP_ENV=local — it is a placeholder while decision D5 is open.
# @name login
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/dev-login
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "email": "ada@example.com",
  "displayName": "Ada Lovelace"
}

### Sign in as a second user, to have someone to share the demo workspace with.
# @name loginGrace
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/dev-login
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "email": "grace@example.com",
  "displayName": "Grace Hopper"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Using the access token
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### The signed-in user's profile.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/users/me
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### The claims on the current token — no database read.
# `wsp` is the one workspace this token is scoped to; `roles` is the role in
# that workspace only.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/auth/userinfo
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Workspaces Ada belongs to, with her role in each. This is what the workspace
# switcher reads. Signing in lands her in the one she owns.
# @name workspaces
GET {{host}}/api/v1/workspaces
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.$.accessToken}}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Refresh and rotation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Trade the refresh token for a new pair. The old refresh token is spent by
# this call; the new access token stays in the same workspace.
# @name refreshed
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "refreshToken": "{{login.response.body.$.refreshToken}}"
}

### Replay the token that was just spent. Expect 401 — and this does more than
# refuse: reuse means a copy is in circulation, so the whole rotation family is
# revoked. Run the request below afterwards to see the *newest* token dead too.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "refreshToken": "{{login.response.body.$.refreshToken}}"
}

### The token issued by the successful refresh above. On its own this would work;
# after the replay it is 401, because the family went down with it.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "refreshToken": "{{refreshed.response.body.$.refreshToken}}"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Switching workspace
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Ada's session is burnt after the replay above, so start a fresh one.
# @name freshLogin
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/dev-login
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "email": "ada@example.com"
}

### Re-scope the session to another workspace. Same rotation as /refresh — the
# only difference is the `wsp` and `roles` claims on the new token.
#
# `items[0]` is whichever workspace is listed first. To watch the role change
# from "owner" to "member", paste the id of "CollabHub Demo" from the
# /api/v1/workspaces response above, then decode the token at jwt.io.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/switch-workspace
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "refreshToken": "{{freshLogin.response.body.$.refreshToken}}",
  "workspaceId": "{{workspaces.response.body.$.items[0].id}}"
}

### Try to switch into a workspace you are not a member of. Expect 403.
# The target comes from the request, so it is only ever trusted after a
# membership lookup against the refresh token's own user — holding someone
# else's workspace id gets you nothing.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/switch-workspace
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "refreshToken": "{{loginGrace.response.body.$.refreshToken}}",
  "workspaceId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing out
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### A session to sign out of.
# @name sessionToEnd
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/dev-login
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "email": "ada@example.com"
}

### Still valid — expect 200.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/users/me
Authorization: Bearer {{sessionToEnd.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Sign out. Expect 204. The access token stays cryptographically valid until it
# expires — that is what stateless verification means — so this writes its `jti`
# to the denylist in R1 with a TTL of whatever life it had left. Passing the
# refresh token revokes that too.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/logout
Content-Type: {{json}}
Authorization: Bearer {{sessionToEnd.response.body.$.accessToken}}

{
  "refreshToken": "{{sessionToEnd.response.body.$.refreshToken}}"
}

### The same token, now on the denylist. Expect 401.
# `docker compose exec redis-cache redis-cli --scan --pattern 'auth:revoked:*'`
# shows the entry.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/users/me
Authorization: Bearer {{sessionToEnd.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### The refresh token that was handed to logout. Expect 401.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "refreshToken": "{{sessionToEnd.response.body.$.refreshToken}}"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Service tokens — how background work calls other services
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Client-credentials grant. The secret comes from AUTH_SERVICE_CLIENTS in .env.
# The token that comes back carries `aud: collabhub-internal`, `sub:
# service:worker` and `scp` scopes, with no workspace at all — decode it and
# compare with a user token.
# @name serviceToken
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/service-token
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "grantType": "client_credentials",
  "clientId": "worker",
  "clientSecret": "worker-local-secret"
}

### A service token on a user endpoint. Expect 401 — the audience check that runs
# on every request is the whole boundary between background work and a user's
# session. It works in the other direction too: a user token can never satisfy
# an /api/v1/internal/ endpoint.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/users/me
Authorization: Bearer {{serviceToken.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Wrong secret. Expect 401.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/service-token
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "grantType": "client_credentials",
  "clientId": "worker",
  "clientSecret": "not-the-secret"
}

### Unknown client. Expect 401 — and the same message as the wrong secret above,
# because which of the two failed is useful only to someone guessing.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/service-token
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "grantType": "client_credentials",
  "clientId": "ghost",
  "clientSecret": "anything"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The error envelope
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### No token. Expect 401 as application/problem+json (RFC 7807), with a `traceId`
# that ties the response to the log line and the trace.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/users/me

### A field that fails validation. Expect 400 with an `errors` map.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/dev-login
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "email": "not-an-email-address"
}

### snake_case where camelCase is expected. Expect 400: JSON on this platform is
# camelCase everywhere, and accepting both spellings would leave two wire
# formats in circulation that no document describes.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "refresh_token": "def456"
}

### An unsupported grant type. Expect 400.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/service-token
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "grantType": "password",
  "clientId": "worker",
  "clientSecret": "worker-local-secret"
}

### Send a trace id and get it back on the problem document — this is how an error
# a user reports leads to the trace in Tempo and the logs in Loki.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/users/me
traceparent: 00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01
